course: dictionary
lesson: 15559
title: Asking humbly: supplication
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Supplication is a noun. It means asking for something humbly and seriously, often from God or from a powerful person. Read the clues in this sentence: «The villagers knelt in supplication before the king, begging him to save their harvest.» Knelt and begging show the asking is humble and serious.
explain: Church and old stories use this word a lot: «The monk's prayers of supplication filled the quiet chapel.» Prayers is a clue that supplication is a kind of prayer. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Asking, pleading or praying humbly all fit, so supplication is a formal word for those.
explain: Supplication is a formal word, so you will meet it more in church, history and older books than in everyday talk. Its plural is supplications, and «in supplication» is a common phrase meaning while pleading humbly.
fact: Supplication is a noun.
fact: «Supplication» means asking for something humbly and seriously.
fact: In church, a supplication is a humble prayer asking God for help.
fact: «In supplication» means pleading humbly, often kneeling or with hands raised.
fact: Supplication can be countable (a supplication, supplications) or uncountable (supplication).
exercise: «The villagers knelt in supplication before the king.» What does supplication mean here? | Playing a game in the square | *Asking humbly and seriously | Shouting angrily at the king | Sleeping on the floor
exercise: The priest read the prayers of supplication in church. What is a supplication in church? | A loud song for a party | A kind of church bell | A list of hymn numbers | *A humble prayer asking God for help
exercise: «He made a supplication with bowed head and folded hands.» Find and copy the word that names the asking. | hands | made | head | *supplication
test: «The prisoner wrote a letter of supplication to the queen, begging for mercy.» What does supplication mean? | A piece of cloth | Angry shouting | A happy story | *Humble, serious asking
test: Which phrase means pleading humbly, often kneeling? | *In supplication | At the seashore | Out of breath | Hymns of praise
exam: Why might an author write «in supplication» instead of «asking»? | Because it means something silly | *Because it is a formal word that shows the asker is humble and serious | Because it means very quick | Because it is only used in sport
